  "student_id": 1,
  "lecture_id": 2,
  "attendance_date": "2026-09-25",
  "status": "present"
}

The backend will validate the request, check the teacher's authorization, and save the attendance record.

Important: The teacher ID should come from the verified JWT rather than being trusted from the request body.

4. Authentication Flow

This section explains how login works.

Student or teacher sends email and password.
Backend searches for the user in MySQL.

bcrypt.compare() checks the password.

Backend generates a JWT token.
Frontend sends the token with protected requests.

authMiddleware verifies the token.

Example authorization header:

Authorization: Bearer YOUR_JWT_TOKEN

You have already worked with JWT authentication in your user-auth-api project, so this flow will be familiar.

5. Attendance Marking Flow

This explains the internal process when a teacher marks attendance.

Teacher selects Lecture 1 and a date.
Frontend displays the student list.
Teacher selects Present or Absent.
Frontend sends the attendance data to the backend.
JWT middleware verifies the teacher.
Backend validates the student, lecture, and attendance status.
Database saves the attendance record.
Backend sends a response to the frontend.
